# TODO - Replaceable accelerator boundary

## Objective

Define a hardware-neutral interface for candidate evaluation, batch VM
execution, search, and verification. Compiler and verifier code must not depend
directly on CUDA APIs.

## Acceptance

- Compiler/verifier code depends on a hardware-neutral capability contract;
  CUDA/ROCm/CPU adapters can be substituted without changing compiler semantics
  or candidate validity rules.
- A CPU/reference path remains sufficient for correctness, and accelerator
  failure/unavailability changes performance rather than semantic acceptance.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `accelerator/`, `algorithms/`,
  `optimizer/`, `benchmarks/accelerator/`, `tests/optimizer/`.
- Required evidence: CPU/reference differential results, device/resource
  metadata, failure/fallback tests, and benchmark samples for claimed speedups.
- Active foundation: `ExactPrimitiveAdapter` owns hardware-neutral classic
  rotate/crazy batches; CPU scalar and optional CUDA adapters implement the same
  request/result contract. `ClassicStepRequest`/`ClassicStepResult` additionally
  define one compact specification-mode VM transition, while
  `ClassicRunRequest`/`ClassicRunResult` define complete classic bounded execution,
  while `ProfileRunGeometry` and the scalable run request/result contract extend
  the boundary to validated ternary profile geometry without exposing CUDA APIs.
  Shared validation rejects malformed requests before backend execution.
- Current differential evidence executes CUDA on available hardware over boundary
  values plus deterministic 4,096-element corpora. Rust differential evidence
  covers compact classic transitions plus resident classic and current-profile
  complete-state bounded runs. A live five-trit/243-word CUDA test additionally
  proves the generated resident kernel is not fixed to canonical 10/14-trit
  geometry. Hardware-neutral Rust batch ports route both real resident CUDA
  workers while safe Rust owns admission and fallback. Reported variants retain
  actual per-item execution origin and live CUDA product tests require at least
  one accepted backend completion, so configured acceleration cannot be confused
  with complete CPU fallback. RTX 4060 current-profile
  baseline, shared-initialization, and persistent-session evidence is also
  retained. Geometry-bound `ProfileMemoryImage` inputs now carry reusable
  validation proof without weakening mutable-input checks. Hardware-neutral
  candidate evaluation, search, verification-assist, and trusted-admission ports
  now have CPU fallback/shape-failure tests. Exact crazy/rotate candidate
  evaluation additionally runs through the same bridge on CPU and live CUDA;
  malformed primitive capability/count/domain evidence falls back safely. Exact
  candidate evidence can also traverse a live CUDA verification-assist adapter;
  hints remain untrusted and malformed optional evidence becomes no hint. Direct
  snapshot materialization removes redundant packed host staging.
  `EvaluatedSearchExecutionAdapter` now composes bounded candidate evaluation into
  search without allowing selectors to fabricate unevaluated payloads. The same
  `classic-rotate-target-search-v1` strategy runs through CPU and live CUDA; CUDA
  matches CPU proposals over 257 candidates while independent CPU admission
  remains authoritative. A retained full-domain comparison over 59,049 candidates
  records a 401.185 ms CPU median and 412.570 ms CUDA median on the RTX 4060,
  preserving the negative 0.972x CUDA/CPU result. The phase profile explains
  97.5% of CPU and 99.5% of CUDA median total time through named phases; CUDA is
  about 57.0% host-side, and batch construction plus proposal selection consume
  about 173.081 ms. Reusable prepared search state is now a neutral extension:
  immutable validated request/batch state is bound to exact strategy identity and
  can cross matching CPU/CUDA capacity without changing admission authority.
  Forged or mismatched state fails closed. Retained repeated-search evidence
  records 1.976x CPU and 1.886x CUDA same-backend prepared improvements, while
  prepared CUDA remains about 9.5% slower than prepared CPU. Preparation is outside
  timed intervals. The prepared phase profile attributes 79.9% of CPU and 81.2%
  of CUDA median total time to backend evaluation. Fixed-width packed candidate
  evidence now implements the next neutral boundary with request-order identity,
  item-form compatibility, fail-closed shape checks, and explicit materialization.
  Retained packed evidence lowers all four route medians; backend evaluation falls
  2.326x on CPU and 2.058x on CUDA. Packed CUDA prepared remains about 18.0%
  slower than CPU. Request decode/validation and primitive transfer are the next
  neutral boundary. Synthesis/guided search,
  resident search, ROCm work ports/VM execution,
  asynchronous submission, and broader hardware remain open.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `batch-vm-execution`,
  `compiler-algorithm-experimentation-platform`.
